WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.679
In this lesson we will look at another kind

2
00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:06.520
of product of vectors: the cross product.

3
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:12.720
The result of the cross product of vectors is
a vector.

4
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:19.140
Before defining the cross product, we need to
understand the orientation

5
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.445
of an ordered triple of vectors a,

6
00:00:21.445 --> 00:00:25.920
b and c in three-dimensional space. For this
we need a new notion.

7
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:33.330
Vectors lying in one plane or in parallel
planes are called coplanar.

8
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:41.510
Let us draw the noncoplanar vectors a, b and c
from one point.

9
00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.623
Depending on the directions of the vectors,

10
00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:48.460
they form a right-handed or a left-handed
triple. If,

11
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.403
looking from the end of the third vector c,

12
00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:55.440
we watch the first vector a turn toward the
second vector b

13
00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:02.130
along the shortest way, and this turn is
counterclockwise,

14
00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:07.436
then the vectors a, b, c form a right-handed

15
00:01:07.436 --> 00:01:12.380
triple. If instead the turn is clockwise,

16
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:19.180
the vectors a, b, c are called a left-handed
triple. In other words,

17
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:26.180
noncoplanar ordered vectors a, b and c with a
common starting point

18
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:33.000
form a triple. If, looking from the end of the
third vector,

19
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.270
we see the shortest turn of the first vector
toward the second

20
00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:43.200
going counterclockwise, the triple is called
right-handed,

21
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.340
and if it goes clockwise, left-handed.

22
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:54.560
Now let us define the cross product. The cross
product of two noncollinear vectors a

23
00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.880
and b is a third vector c

24
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.990
that satisfies these conditions. Condition
one:

25
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:09.310
vector c is orthogonal to each of the vectors
a and b,

26
00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:14.260
that is, it is perpendicular to the plane
through a and b.

27
00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.547
Condition two: the length of vector c equals
the area

28
00:02:17.547 --> 00:02:20.400
of the parallelogram built on vectors a and b,

29
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:28.074
that is, the magnitude of c equals the product
of the

30
00:02:28.074 --> 00:02:34.880
magnitudes of a and b and the sine of the
angle

31
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.330
between them.

32
00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:44.530
Condition three: the vectors a, b and c form
an ordered right-handed triple.

33
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:48.910
The cross product is written like this.
Remember:

34
00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.830
the sign of the cross product is a cross.

35
00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:58.430
Do not confuse it with the dot product sign:
these are different operations.

36
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.780
Now let us look at the properties of the cross
product.

37
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:10.320
Property one: the cross product is not
commutative.

38
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.940
Property two: if the cross product of vectors
equals the zero vector,

39
00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.920
then vectors a and b are collinear.

40
00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:28.680
Property three: it is associative with respect
to a scalar factor.

41
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:36.060
Property four: the cross product is also
distributive.

42
00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.120
Since any vector has definite coordinates in a
coordinate system,

43
00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.975
we can introduce a second definition of the
cross product,

44
00:03:48.975 --> 00:03:55.420
which lets us find its coordinates from the
given coordinates of the vectors.

45
00:03:55.420 --> 00:04:02.979
The cross product can be viewed as the
determinant of a third-order

46
00:04:02.979 --> 00:04:08.620
square matrix. In a rectangular coordinate
system,

47
00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.188
the first row holds the unit vectors i,

48
00:04:12.188 --> 00:04:20.240
j, k, the second the coordinates of vector a,
and the third the coordinates of vector b.

49
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.790
Computing the determinant by expansion,

50
00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.810
we get the cross product expressed in
coordinates.

51
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.468
Now let us look at an example with the cross
product.

52
00:04:35.468 --> 00:04:37.970
In a rectangular coordinate system,

53
00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:40.248
vector a has coordinates three,

54
00:04:40.248 --> 00:04:44.950
four, five and vector b has coordinates four,
five, minus three.

55
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:51.570
We need to find the cross product of vectors a
and b.

56
00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:58.460
To do this, we form a third-order determinant
and expand it along the first row.

57
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:07.570
We get the sum of the products of the
first-row entries and their cofactors.

58
00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:14.270
Let us find the values of the second-order
determinants. So the cross product

59
00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.977
of vectors a and b is the vector with
coordinates

60
00:05:17.977 --> 00:05:21.230
minus thirty-seven, twenty-nine, minus one.

61
00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.299
Using the cross product, we can find the area

62
00:05:25.299 --> 00:05:28.750
of the parallelogram built on these vectors,

63
00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:34.420
and therefore the area of a triangle too.

64
00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.660
Let us look at an example of finding the area
of a triangle

65
00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.300
using the cross product. In a rectangular

66
00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:49.080
Cartesian coordinate system, a triangle ABC is
given. Using the cross product,

67
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.860
let us find the area of triangle ABC.

68
00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:58.440
To do this, we find the coordinates of vectors
AB and AC. For that

69
00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.304
we compute the differences of the
corresponding coordinates

70
00:06:02.304 --> 00:06:05.120
of the end and start points of each vector.

71
00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:11.820
Now we form a third-order determinant from
their coordinates and find

72
00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:18.550
the cross product. We get a vector with
coordinates six, minus four, minus six.

73
00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:24.930
We compute the length of the cross product
from its coordinates and get

74
00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.568
the square root of eighty-eight.

75
00:06:27.568 --> 00:06:32.020
The result is two times the square root of
twenty-two.

76
00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:38.100
Now we can find the area of the triangle.
Substituting the results into the formula,

77
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.886
after simplifying we get that the area of the
triangle

78
00:06:41.886 --> 00:06:44.480
equals the square root of twenty-two.

79
00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:49.720
In this lesson we learned what the cross
product is,

80
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:57.400
listed its properties and learned how to find
the cross product.
